      toaster.js                  transient toasts
      settings.js                 OS-notification opt-in, reminder lead time

  [1mTask:[0m [38;5;153m{ id, title, status: 'open' | 'done', dueAt: ISO | null, watched: boolean, createdAt, updatedAt }[39m

  [1mNotification:[0m [38;5;153m{ id, kind: 'due-soon' | 'overdue' | 'feedback', taskId | null, title, body, createdAt, readAt, [39m
  [38;5;153mdedupeKey, transient }[39m

  Two decisions in there worth flagging explicitly, because they're the ones I'd want you to push back on:

  [1m[38;5;153mrules.js[39m is a pure function.[0m Given a task list, a timestamp, and the set of reminder keys already fired, it returns
  the notifications that should now exist. It touches no timers, no DOM, no storage. The scheduler is a dumb shell that
  calls it on an interval, on task change, and when the tab becomes visible. This is deliberate: the reminder logic is
  the only genuinely tricky part of this feature, and this shape lets it be tested by passing in a fake [38;5;153mnow[39m instead of
  waiting for clocks.

  [1m"Shared surface" means one queue, not one lifetime.[0m Both reminders and action feedback go through a single [38;5;153mnotify()[39m
  entry point and a single store. But reminders persist in the tray until dismissed, while action feedback is [38;5;153mtransient:
[39m  [38;5;153mtrue[39m — it shows as a toast, auto-expires, and is never archived. An activity log of "Task saved" entries would be
  noise, and a tray you have to clear out constantly is a tray people stop opening.

  Storage is three versioned localStorage keys ([38;5;153mtasks.v1[39m, [38;5;153mnotifications.v1[39m, [38;5;153msettings.v1[39m). If localStorage is unavailable
  (private browsing) or holds corrupt JSON, we fall back to in-memory, tell the user once, and keep running rather than
  failing to boot.
